    "original_title": "One Swim That Could Save a Life: Children in Mogadishu Learning Water Safety.",
    "slug": "mogadishu-children-learn-water-safety-at-liido-beach",
    "summary": "A free swimming and water-safety program for children under 15 is running twice weekly at Liido Beach in Mogadishu, aiming to reduce drowning deaths. The campaign teaches swimming basics and rescue skills, led by Asha Geesdiir.",
    "content": "In Mogadishu, children under the age of 15 are participating in a free water-safety initiative at Liido Beach, held two days each week. The program, led by Asha Geesdiir, was developed in response to the frequent drowning incidents affecting young people along the Somali coast. It provides both boys and girls with practical swimming lessons and guidance on how to stay safe in the water, as well as how to help others in distress without endangering themselves.\n\nThe curriculum covers essential swimming strokes, recognizing hazardous conditions, and techniques for assisting a person who has fallen into the water. Instructors emphasize the importance of calm and careful actions to prevent further risk. The initiative reflects a broader effort by local communities and organizations to address a public safety concern that often goes unnoticed in urban settings.\n\nDrowning is a leading cause of accidental death among children in many coastal regions, and Somalia is no exception. With limited formal water-safety education, many children learn to swim by trial or avoid water altogether, leaving them vulnerable. This program seeks to change that by offering structured, accessible training that builds both skill and confidence.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this initiative holds particular significance. Many diaspora families maintain strong ties to Mogadishu and may visit or send their children during holidays. Knowing that such programs exist provides reassurance that children can enjoy the beach with reduced risk. Moreover, the diaspora often supports local development projects, and water-safety education is a practical investment in community well-being.\n\nThe campaign has already drawn interest from parents and local leaders, who see it as a vital step toward reducing preventable tragedies. As the program continues, organizers hope to expand its reach, potentially involving more instructors and partnering with schools. For now, each session at Liido Beach represents a concrete effort to equip Mogadishu’s children with life-saving skills.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/08/29/one-swim-that-could-save-a-life-children-in-mogadishu-learning-water-safety/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=one-swim-that-could-save-a-life-children-in-mogadishu-learning-water-safety",
