    "title": "Somalia Prepares First New Shilling Banknotes in 35 Years",
    "original_title": "Somalia to Issue New Currency for First Time in 35 Years",
    "slug": "somalia-prepares-first-new-shilling-banknotes-in-35-years",
    "summary": "Somalia's central bank is set to issue new shilling banknotes for the first time since 1991, aiming to modernize the currency and restore monetary sovereignty. The move comes after traders in Mogadishu began rejecting old notes due to their poor condition.",
    "content": "Somalia's central bank is preparing to introduce new shilling banknotes for the first time in 35 years, a significant step toward overhauling a financial system that has relied on heavily worn notes. Officials confirmed that preparations have reached an advanced stage, with the new currency expected to replace the aging banknotes that have been in circulation since before the civil war.\n\nThe decision follows growing difficulties in Mogadishu, where traders have started refusing to accept the old notes because they are badly deteriorated and difficult to authenticate. By issuing fresh currency, the government aims to restore confidence in the Somali shilling and reassert monetary authority that has been fragmented for decades.\n\nParliament is currently reviewing legislation to establish a Currency Board, a body that would strengthen the central bank's capacity to manage the money supply and stabilize the currency. The central bank is also working on measures to ensure the new notes are secure and durable, addressing concerns about counterfeiting and wear.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development is particularly meaningful, as it signals progress in rebuilding national institutions that many have not seen function effectively. Remittances from abroad have long sustained the economy, and a more reliable currency could facilitate smoother transactions for diaspora families supporting relatives at home.\n\nThe introduction of new banknotes is also part of broader efforts to modernize the financial sector, which has operated largely without a functioning central bank since the early 1990s. While challenges remain, including political hurdles and the need for widespread public acceptance, the move represents a concrete step toward economic stabilization.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/08/29/somalia-to-issue-new-currency-for-first-time-in-35-years/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=somalia-to-issue-new-currency-for-first-time-in-35-years",
