    "id": "news-1788004735899-3",
    "title": "Uganda Sets August 2027 Date for Troop Withdrawal from Somalia",
    "original_title": "Uganda Troop Withdrawal From Somalia Set for 2027",
    "slug": "uganda-sets-august-2027-date-for-troop-withdrawal-from-somalia",
    "summary": "Uganda's army chief announced on Friday that all Ugandan troops will leave Somalia by August 1, 2027, ending nearly two decades of military involvement. The withdrawal comes amid funding challenges for the African Union mission in Somalia.",
    "content": "Uganda's military leadership has announced a definitive timeline for the end of its long-running deployment in Somalia. Army chief General Muhoozi Kainerugaba stated on social media that all Ugandan forces will be withdrawn from the country by August 1, 2027. The announcement, made on Friday, August 28, 2026, marks a significant milestone in what has been one of the longest-standing peace support operations in the region.\n\nUganda first deployed troops to Somalia in 2007 as part of the African Union mission, initially to protect the federal government and key infrastructure from the armed group al-Shabab. Over the years, Uganda has been one of the largest contributors of troops to the mission, which has evolved from AMISOM to the current African Union Support and Stabilization Mission in Somalia (AUSSOM). The withdrawal announcement comes as the mission grapples with a funding crisis, particularly after the United States signaled it would cease financial backing, putting pressure on other contributing nations.\n\nThe timing of the withdrawal is critical, as Somali forces are still in the process of taking over full security responsibility from international troops. The transition has been a gradual one, with several phases of handover planned. Ugandan officials have not detailed the exact timeline of the drawdown, but the August 2027 deadline provides a clear end date for the mission's longest-serving contributor.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development carries particular significance. Many diaspora members have family and business connections in regions that have benefited from the relative stability provided by Ugandan troops. The withdrawal could alter the security landscape, potentially affecting plans for investment, travel, and eventual return to Somalia. Moreover, the diaspora has watched closely as international support for Somalia's stabilization has fluctuated, and this announcement underscores the need for continued engagement with Somali-led security efforts.\n\nAs the 2027 deadline approaches, the focus will shift to how Somali forces fill the vacuum, and whether the international community can provide sustained support to prevent al-Shabab from regaining ground. The coming months will be pivotal in determining the durability of security gains made over nearly two decades.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/08/29/uganda-troop-withdrawal-from-somalia/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=uganda-troop-withdrawal-from-somalia",
