    "slug": "new-levy-halts-sand-transport-work-for-displaced-families-in-baidoa",
    "summary": "A new payment demanded by Al-Shabaab from sand transporters in Baidoa has forced hundreds of displaced workers to stop work, leaving families without income since July.",
    "content": "In Baidoa, a city in southwestern Somalia, a newly imposed levy by the militant group Al-Shabaab has brought sand transport work to a standstill, affecting hundreds of displaced laborers who rely on this activity for their daily survival. The tax, introduced in July 2026, requires vehicle owners to pay additional sums for extracting and transporting sand from sites outside the city, which is used in construction. Workers say they cannot afford the new payments and have been forced to abandon their jobs.\n\nJamal Hassan Mohamed, a father of ten, is among those affected. He reported that he has been unable to provide enough food for his family since the levy was introduced. He extracts sand at sites outside Baidoa and transports it to the city for construction purposes. According to Mohamed, individuals controlling the areas outside the city have effectively prohibited the work unless the tax is paid, leaving him and others with no alternative income.\n\nThe disruption comes at a time when many displaced families in Baidoa already face severe economic hardship, with limited access to stable employment and rising food prices. The sand transport sector has historically provided a vital income source for internally displaced people (IDPs), many of whom fled conflict and drought in other regions of Somalia. The new tax threatens to deepen their vulnerability and worsen food insecurity.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development highlights the persistent challenges facing their relatives and communities in Somalia. Many diaspora members send remittances to support families in Baidoa, and disruptions in local livelihoods increase their financial burden. The situation calls for attention from humanitarian organizations and Somali authorities to address the impact of such informal taxes and support alternative income-generating opportunities for affected populations.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Ergo here: https://radioergo.org/en/2026/08/new-tax-halts-lifeline-work-for-displaced-sand-transporters-in-baidoa/",
    "source": "radioergo.org",
